<section class="about-story">

  <p>
    Hi,
    <br/>
    I'm <a class="text-link" href="mailto:contact@bakelorium.com">Nathaniel Perry</a>,
    a software engineer based in New Hampshire.

    I've been working on this project for a while.

    My first commit on this project was
    <time class="bolded" datetime="2025-11-11">Nov, 11 2025</time>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Around when I turned 40,
    I started reacting more severely to Milk and Eggs.

    I have to tell every waiter, check every ingredient list.

    It's a chore that gets exhausting.
  </p>
  <p>I'm hoping to change that.</p>

  <p>
    I am one person, and I prefer quality-over-quantity,
    so you will have to be patient.
    </p>

  <p>
    The app data is curated from real scientific research,
    with sources cited.

    No mystery where that data came from.

    Plus an in-app dictionary, so you'll be able
    to look up these cooking and baking terms
  </p>

  <p>
    I cook often, and have made a lot of different dishes.

    My cooking style makes substitutions come naturally,
    but I know others out there don't share my instinct.

    You'll be able to choose ingredient substitutions
    while you follow the recipe.
  </p>

  <p>
    I want to build an app that will work for you for the foreseeable future.

    Something that will still work even without my cloud services.
    With features like offline direct phone-to-phone sharing, so no server needs to be involved.
  </p>

  <p>
    I designed a new file format, BakeBundle,
    and to keep you from getting trapped in my format,
    I plan to publish it and leave it open for others to implement importers.

    In addition, I want to allow exporting recipes to many common formats.
  </p>

  <p>
    I use AI coding assistants the way I use a compiler and a linter.

    A useful tool and collaboration muse to build out features.
  </p>

